extraction_notes: "WB20 Canada printed 88-90 (cache p089-p091, cache page = printed folio + 1), read off the text layer; all three pages were rendered and read beside it. Heading Trapper-Woodsman OCC on printed 88, after the pointer list Other Common O.C.C.s available to Tundra Rangers; Special Skills & O.C.C. Abilities and the stat block headed The North American Trapper-Woodsman O.C.C. (also known as Woodsman, Trapper and Furrier) on printed 89; the entry ends on printed 90 with a note that this is a different class from the Huntsman-Trapper of Warlords of Russia. Southwestern Canada starts on printed 91. || GROUP: the book has no O.C.C. group headings. The entry says the Trapper-Woodsman is not a Tundra Ranger and calls the character a rogue, mercenary or independent entrepreneur, so occ_group is optional and men_of_arms is false, as for the Huntsman-Trapper. The entry prints no S.D.C. base; +4D6+10 S.D.C. is listed under O.C.C. Bonuses and is stored as a pool bonus. || XP: the Tundra Ranger, Headhunter Techno-Hound and Trapper-Woodsman ladder on printed 192, as supplied from a render. || BONUSES: +2 initiative, +3 pull punch, +1 vs possession stored; +2 vs poison and disease is toxins_poisons 2 and disease 2; +2 vs cold and pain is pain 2 and a saves.other vs cold 2; +1 vs Horror Factor at levels 1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13 and 15 is horror_factor 1 flat for level 1 plus seven at_level entries. || SPECIAL ABILITIES: Track and trap monstrous animals (20%/30% +5% per level) and Forest-wise (26% +4% per level) are special_abilities prose; neither has a catalog skill row. The O.C.C. skill list separately prints Track Animals (+20%), stored as Track & Trap Animals at 40. || LANGUAGES: Speaks American (English) at 92% is Language: Native Tongue at 92. Speaks Inuit and one other language of choice (+15%): the catalog has no Language: Inuit row, so the line is stored as two Language: Other picks at +15%. The entry prints no literacy. || SKILL READINGS: Basic Math is Mathematics: Basic; Lore: Cattle/Animals is Lore: Cattle & Animals; Identify Plants & Fruits is Identify Plants & Fruit; Swim and Climb are Swimming and Climbing. W.P. two ancient of choice and W.P. one M.D. energy weapon of choice are free Weapon Proficiencies picks with the limit in the note. Hand to Hand: Expert, no others available. || RELATED: four at level one and two more at levels 4, 8 and 12. Espionage none except Detect Ambush, Detect Concealment, Intelligence and Tracking (+5%), Tracking being Tracking (people). Science is Astronomy, Botany and Mathematics: Advanced (+5%). Technical any +10% with Whittling, Breeding Dogs and Falconry at +15%: Technical is stored at the flat +10%, so Whittling & Sculpting and Breed Dogs are 5% short and the note says to add it; Falconry is filed under Military in the catalog, so Military appears with only Falconry at +15% although the book says Military: None. Pilot except robots, power armor, military vehicles, ships and aircraft excludes the robot and power armor rows, every Military: pilot row, Boat: Ships and Boat: Submersibles, and every aircraft and spacecraft row. Most opt for Horsemanship and/or Ride Exotic Animals is the catalog Horsemanship category limited to General and Exotic Animals. || MONEY: the book prints 4D4x100 credits worth of furs, horns, bone, nuggets of silver or gold and other tradeable goods (text layer 4D4xlOO); that is goods, not coin, so starting_money is 0 and the goods are in GM Notes. || EQUIPMENT: the light M.D.C. armor is a pick between the homemade light armor row and partial Fury Beetle armor; two canteens or water skins on the canteen row; duffle bag or saddlebag as a pick; skinning knives print no number and two are stored; animal snares print no number and one is stored. NOT stored: one weapon for each W.P. (unnamed), cooking utensils, the two boxes of 100 sealable plastic bags, 1D4 airtight plastic containers, fur cloak or long coat, 20 feet of wire and a handful of personal items have no catalog row. Secret Resources: none per se."
---

## Lore

The Trapper-Woodsman is no Tundra Ranger, but the Rangers meet them all the
time. They are the hunters, trappers and furriers of the Canadian wilds, the
New West and the forests of the eastern United States: rough, loud,
self-reliant people who are far more comfortable under trees than on a city
street. Hunting, fishing, snaring and skinning are how they earn a living,
trading pelts, horn, claws, teeth and meat with Native Americans, trading
posts, farms and anyone they meet on the trail.

Most like the woods because nobody there tells them how to behave. They
belch, swear and say what they think, and they enjoy it when city people
take them for ignorant savages; plenty of them play the bumpkin on purpose
around Coalition soldiers and strangers they distrust. Under the act they
are sharp, observant and quick, and they like a surprise and a challenge.

They also like adventure and a good fight, and they have a soft spot they
will not admit to. A Trapper who takes a liking to someone is fiercely
loyal, and most will stop to help women, children and lawmen, pass news and
warnings to the Rangers, and now and then ride with them against something
evil. Few accept bionics. They wear Mega-Damage armor, often homemade or cut
from Fury Beetle shell, and carry both M.D. weapons and ordinary hunting
rifles or bows.

## GM Notes

Starting kit the sheet does not list: one weapon for each W.P. (1D4+2 extra
ammo-clips for each are on the sheet as E-Clips), cooking utensils, a box of
100 sealable plastic sandwich bags, a box of 100 large sealable bags, 1D4
airtight resealable plastic containers, a fur cloak or long coat, wire (20
feet) and a few personal items. More weapons, special items and magic
items come with time and role-playing.

Money: no cash. The character starts with 4D4x100 credits worth of furs,
horns, bone, silver or gold nuggets and other trade goods (herbs, spices,
birds, small animals). Most spend quickly and live off the land.

Languages: Inuit and one language of choice, each at +15%, stored as two
free language picks.

Technical skills: Whittling & Sculpting and Breed Dogs get +15% rather than
the +10% the sheet applies; add 5% by hand if either is taken as a related
skill.

Secret resources: none as such, though most Trappers are on good terms with
the trading posts, a favorite town and the Tundra Rangers.

This is a different class from the Huntsman-Trapper of Warlords of Russia.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'trapper-woodsman');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
-- these, and a CR in the stored markdown means the checkout mangled the file.
SELECT class_id, name, status, length(markdown) AS bytes, instr(markdown, char(13)) > 0 AS has_cr
  FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'trapper-woodsman';

-- Records this run. REQUIRED: the smoke test fails a data script that has no
-- footer, or whose footer names a different file.
INSERT INTO data_script_runs (filename) VALUES ('add-trapper-woodsman-class.sql');
